Dimension Tables:

A dimension table stores descriptive, contextual data that gives meaning to facts.
It answers questions like Who, What, Where, When, and How about a business event.

When

Dimension tables are used whenever analytical reporting or OLAP analysis is required, especially in Data Warehouses and BI systems.

Why

Dimension tables are needed to:

Make reports human-readable
Enable filtering, grouping, and slicing
Improve query performance
Support historical tracking (Slowly Changing Dimensions)

Where

Dimension tables exist in:

Data Warehouses
Data Marts
OLAP cubes
They are joined to fact tables.

How

Designed with one primary key (surrogate key)
Contain business attributes
Change slowly over time
Linked to fact tables using foreign keys

Example

Patient Dimension (Dim_Patient)

Patient_SK	MRN	Patient_Name	Gender	DOB
101	MRN123	John Doe	M	1985-04-10
Healthcare Real-Life Scenario

In our healthcare data warehouse, I worked on the Patient, Provider, Facility, and Date dimensions. These dimensions were linked to fact tables such as Fact_Encounters and Fact_Claims. Business users used the Patient and Provider dimensions to filter reports by age group, gender, specialty, or location, while historical changes like patient address updates were tracked using Slowly Changing Dimensions (Type 2) to ensure accurate reporting and compliance.
