Fact Tables
What

A fact table stores measurable business events and numeric data that can be aggregated and analyzed.
It answers questions like How many? How much? How often?

When

Fact tables are used whenever analytical reporting, KPIs, or trend analysis is required in a Data Warehouse.

Why

Fact tables are needed to:

Store business metrics

Enable aggregations (SUM, COUNT, AVG)

Support performance analysis

Act as the central table in analytical queries

Where

Fact tables exist in:

Data Warehouses

Data Marts

OLAP systems
They are connected to multiple dimension tables.

How

Contains foreign keys referencing dimension tables

Stores numeric measures

Usually contains millions or billions of rows

Designed at a specific grain (level of detail)

Example

Fact_Encounter

Patient_SK	Provider_SK	Facility_SK	Date_SK	Visit_Count	Visit_Cost
101	201	301	20240115	1	1200
Healthcare Real-Life Scenario

In our healthcare data warehouse, I worked on Fact_Encounter and Fact_Claims tables at the encounter and claim-line grain. These fact tables captured metrics such as visit counts, charges, reimbursements, and length of stay. Business teams used these facts to analyze cost per patient, provider performance, readmission trends, and revenue metrics across time, facilities, and departments.
