Factless Fact Tables
What

A factless fact table is a fact table that contains no numeric measures.
It only stores foreign keys to dimension tables to record that an event occurred or a condition exists.

Why

Factless fact tables are used to:

Track events without numbers
Capture relationships
Enable count-based analysis
Answer “Did it happen?” questions

When
Used when:
There are no measurable values
Only presence or absence of an event matters
Business wants counts or trend analysis

Where

Found in:
Data Warehouses
Dimensional models
Star schemas

How

Contains only dimension foreign keys
Often includes a Date dimension
Analysis is done using COUNT(*)

Types of Factless Fact Tables

1)Event Tracking Factless Table
Tracks when an event happened.

Healthcare Example

Fact_Patient_Appointment

Patient_SK	Provider_SK	Date_SK
101	201	20240115

Use case:

Count appointments per day
No-show analysis
Provider workload

2)Coverage / Eligibility Factless Table

Tracks conditions over a period.
Healthcare Example

Fact_Insurance_Eligibility

Patient_SK	Insurance_SK	Date_SK
101	401	20240101

Use case:
Which patients were insured on a given date
Eligibility reporting

Healthcare Real-Life Scenario

In our healthcare data warehouse, I worked with factless fact tables to track patient appointments and insurance eligibility. These tables did not store costs or amounts, but allowed the business to count visits, identify missed appointments, analyze provider schedules, and verify patient coverage for compliance and operational reporting.
